<section xml:id="sec-mod9-assessment">
  <title>Assessment</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      This assessment has three parts. Part 1 asks you to write a
      complete mathematical proof, and Part 2 a complete analysis with
      every step justified; both are submitted for feedback. Part 3 is
      a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single
      document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> All proofs must be written in
      complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked
      Examples: announce your strategy, include scratch work (clearly labeled) before the formal proof, write in complete grammatical
      sentences, invoke each theorem by name, and justify every step.
      Work consisting only of symbolic manipulations without explanation
      will not receive full credit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>A note on Part 2.</alert> Part 2 is a single required problem; there are no options to choose between. All students complete the same Part 2 problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Academic integrity.</alert> Your proofs must be your own
      work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked
      examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other
      students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft
      your arguments.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Part 1: Uniform Convergence Preserves Continuity (Required)</alert>
      (Estimated time: 15 minutes)
    </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-as-1">
    <title>The Continuity Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove the following theorem: <em>If <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly
        on <m>[a,b]</m> and each <m>f_n</m> is continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>.</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must have the following structure:
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            <em>Setup.</em> Fix <m>x_0 \in [a,b]</m> and
            <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. State what you need to show
            and identify the three quantities you will bound.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Choose <m>N</m> from uniform convergence.</em>
            Use the definition of uniform convergence to obtain
            <m>N \in \N</m> such that <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)| \lt
            \varepsilon/3</m> for all <m>n \geq N</m> and all
            <m>x \in [a,b]</m>. Say explicitly that you fix one
            such <m>N</m>, and note that it does not depend on
            <m>x</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Choose <m>\delta</m> from continuity of <m>f_N</m>.</em>
            Use continuity of <m>f_N</m> at <m>x_0</m> to obtain
            <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that
            <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f_N(x) - f_N(x_0)|
            \lt \varepsilon/3</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>The triangle inequality.</em> Suppose
            <m>|x - x_0| \lt \delta</m>. Write out the
            <m>3\varepsilon</m> split
            <md>
              |f(x) - f(x_0)| \leq |f(x) - f_N(x)| +
              |f_N(x) - f_N(x_0)| + |f_N(x_0) - f(x_0)|
            </md>
            and bound each term by <m>\varepsilon/3</m>, citing the
            source of each bound.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Conclusion.</em> State why <m>f</m> is continuous
            at <m>x_0</m>, and why this implies <m>f</m> is
            continuous on all of <m>[a,b]</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The critical step is (ii): the <m>N</m> you obtain from
        uniform convergence must not depend on <m>x</m>&#x2014;it
        works simultaneously for all <m>x \in [a,b]</m>. This is
        exactly the content of the quantifier structure of uniform
        convergence, and it is what makes the proof work. If you
        find yourself choosing a different <m>N</m> for each
        <m>x</m>, you are using only pointwise convergence.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Part 2: Analyzing a Non-Uniform Sequence (Required)</alert>
      (Estimated time: 20 minutes)
    </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-as-2">
    <title>The Sequence <m>f_n(x) = n^2x(1-x)^n</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f_n(x) = n^2x(1-x)^n</m> for <m>x \in [0,1]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            <em>Pointwise limit.</em> Compute
            <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} f_n(x)</m> for each fixed
            <m>x \in [0,1]</m>. (Handle <m>x = 0</m>, <m>x = 1</m>, and
            <m>x \in (0,1)</m> separately.) Call the pointwise
            limit <m>f(x)</m> and state it explicitly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Supremum of <m>|f_n - f|</m>.</em> For each
            <m>n</m>, find the maximum of <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>
            over <m>x \in [0,1]</m>. (Use single-variable
            calculus: differentiate, find the critical point in
            <m>(0,1)</m>, and check that it gives the maximum.) Compute
            <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m> and
            determine whether this supremum tends to <m>0</m>
            as <m>n \to \infty</m>. You may use the limit
            <m>(1 + 1/n)^n \to e</m>, which is used in
            <xref ref="ex-mod5-we-3"/>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Uniform convergence verdict.</em> Does
            <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>[0,1]</m>? Justify
            your answer using the result of (ii).
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Comparing integrals.</em> Compute
            <m>\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_0^1 f_n(x)\,dx</m>
            and <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx</m>. Are they equal?
            The term-by-term integration theorem would make them equal
            if its hypotheses held. Which hypothesis of that theorem
            fails for this sequence, and which hypotheses hold? Cite
            your answers to (ii) and (iii).
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For part (i): for fixed <m>x \in (0,1)</m>, compare consecutive
        terms <m>f_{n+1}(x)</m> and <m>f_n(x)</m>, and recall the tests
        for series in <xref ref="ch-mod5"/>.
        For part (ii): differentiate <m>f_n</m> with respect to
        <m>x</m> and factor the derivative.
        For part (iv): find an antiderivative of <m>f_n</m> (the
        substitution <m>u = 1 - x</m> suggests one), confirm it by
        differentiating with the chain rule from
        <xref ref="ch-mod7"/>, and apply
        Part 2 of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus from
        <xref ref="ch-mod8"/>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Part 3: Reflection</alert>
      (Estimated time: 5 minutes)
    </p>
    <p>
      Write 3&#x2013;5 sentences for each question. These are graded
      for thoughtfulness, not for mathematical correctness.
    </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-as-r1">
    <title>The Quantifier Swap</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The only formal difference between pointwise and uniform
        convergence is the order of two quantifiers. In 3&#x2013;5
        sentences, explain in your own words why this single
        quantifier swap has such dramatic consequences&#x2014;why
        does moving <m>\exists N</m> outside the <m>\forall x</m>
        change what properties the limit function inherits?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-as-r2">
    <title>Bauldry &#xa7;2.6 &#x2014; First Impressions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        After reading Bauldry &#xa7;2.6, describe in 3&#x2013;5
        sentences what feels most different about the graduate
        presentation compared to the module material. Was there
        a result in Bauldry that surprised you, or that the module
        did not fully prepare you for?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-as-r3">
    <title>Course Capstone Reflection</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        This is the final assessment of the prep course. In
        4&#x2013;6 sentences, reflect on the following: Which module
        or topic represented the biggest conceptual leap for you?
        What single idea from the course do you expect to use most
        in MAT 5610? Is there any topic from the nine modules that
        you feel you would benefit from revisiting before the
        semester begins?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Assessment Rubric (Informational)</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The following rubric describes how submitted proofs will be
      evaluated.
    </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-as-rubric">
    <title>Grading Criteria</title>
    <tabular halign="left">
      <col width="14%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="28%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Full credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Partial credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Minimal credit</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Logical correctness</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>All steps are valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly correct with one fixable gap</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Significant logical errors or wrong claim proved</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Quantifier structure</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>N</m> chosen before <m>x</m> in Part 1; uniform vs. pointwise distinction explicit throughout</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Correct idea but quantifier order not made explicit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Pointwise and uniform convergence conflated; <m>N</m> depending on <m>x</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Supremum computation</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Critical point found correctly in Part 2 (ii); sup computed and limit evaluated</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Maximum found with minor computational error</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Supremum not computed or wrong method used</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Part 2 analysis</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Pointwise limit in Part 2 (i) correct in all three cases; uniform-convergence verdict in (iii) justified from (ii); both integrals in (iv) computed correctly and the failed hypothesis named, with the hypotheses that hold identified</p></cell>
        <cell><p>One of (i), (iii), (iv) incomplete or with a minor error, or the failed hypothesis named without justification</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Pointwise limit wrong, verdict unsupported, or integrals not computed</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Mathematical prose</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Complete grammatical sentences; strategy stated up front; every step justified</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly prose with some missing connective sentences</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Primarily symbolic with no connecting narrative</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Reflection</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Specific, thoughtful, and engages with the mathematical content and course arc</p></cell>
        <cell><p>General but relevant observations</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Vague or too brief to be informative</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
